INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'nicl-graduate-apprentice-200-posts-20261106', 'asia', 'India',
'NICL Graduate Apprentice Recruitment 2026: 200 Posts, Apply by 18 November',
'एनआईसीएल स्नातक अप्रेन्टिस भर्ती २०२६: २०० पद, १८ नोभेम्बरसम्म आवेदन',
'National Insurance Company Limited has opened 200 Graduate Apprentice seats for 2026-27, with applications running from 4 to 18 November 2026 through the NATS portal. There is no written test and no interview: shortlisting is on graduation marks, and the stipend is Rs 15,000 a month for 12 months.',
'नेसनल इन्स्योरेन्स कम्पनी लिमिटेडले २०० स्नातक अप्रेन्टिस पद खोलेको छ। ४ देखि १८ नोभेम्बर २०२६ सम्म एनएटीएस पोर्टलबाट आवेदन दिन सकिन्छ। मासिक भत्ता १५,००० भारतीय रुपैयाँ छ र छनोट स्नातक अंकका आधारमा हुन्छ।',
$$<p>National Insurance Company Limited has opened 200 Graduate Apprentice seats for 2026-27 under the National Apprenticeship Training Scheme. Applications run from 4 to 18 November 2026, and you apply only through the NATS portal, not on the NICL website. The stipend is Rs 15,000 a month for 12 months, and the age limit is 21 to 30 years as on 1 April 2026. NICL Apprentice Recruitment 2026 is an apprenticeship, not a permanent job, and that distinction shapes every decision below, from who should apply to what the year is worth.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>NICL Graduate Apprentice 2026-27</th></tr><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>National Insurance Company Limited (NICL)</td></tr><tr><td>Scheme</td><td>National Apprenticeship Training Scheme (NATS), Apprentices Act 1961</td></tr><tr><td>Post</td><td>Graduate Apprentice</td></tr><tr><td>Total seats</td><td>200 (provisional)</td></tr><tr><td>States covered</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Notification date</td><td>4 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Application dates</td><td>4 to 18 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Application route</td><td>NATS portal (nats.education.gov.in) only</td></tr><tr><td>NATS establishment ID</td><td>EWBPNC096867</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification</td><td>Regular full-time graduation in any discipline, degree held by 1 April 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Age limit</td><td>21 to 30 years as on 1 April 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Monthly stipend</td><td>Rs 15,000</td></tr><tr><td>Training duration</td><td>12 months</td></tr><tr><td>Application fee</td><td>None named in the notification</td></tr><tr><td>Selection</td><td>Graduation marks, then document verification</td></tr></table>
<h2>Who can apply, and who cannot</h2>
<p>Any graduate can apply, with three conditions attached. The degree must be regular and full-time, from a university or institution recognised by AICTE, DOTE or UGC. You must have passed and held the degree certificate by 1 April 2026. And you must have a 100 percent complete profile on the NATS portal, because only a complete profile can be used to apply.</p>
<p>The notification then lists the people it does not want, and the list is longer than most candidates expect. Postgraduates cannot apply, even if they also hold an eligible bachelor's degree. Anyone who has done an integrated postgraduate course is out too. So is anyone who has already done apprenticeship training, with NICL or anywhere else, anyone currently doing it, and anyone whose earlier apprenticeship was ended midway. One year or more of work experience in a government, public-sector or private organisation as on 1 April 2026 also disqualifies you. Distance-learning and part-time degrees do not meet the regular full-time condition.</p>
<p>The age window is 21 to 30 years as on 1 April 2026. For General and EWS candidates that means a date of birth between 1 April 1996 and 1 April 2005, both dates included. Relaxation follows Government of India rules: 5 years for SC and ST, 3 years for OBC non-creamy layer, and 10 years for PwBD candidates in the General and EWS categories, rising to 13 for PwBD OBC and 15 for PwBD SC and ST. Read the exclusion list before you spend time on the NATS profile. A postgraduate with a strong degree and a year of work experience fails on two counts at once, and the notification gives no way around either.</p>
<h2>The state-wise seats</h2>
<p>NICL has split the 200 seats across 14 states, and you apply for one state only. Assam has the largest share at 30 seats, Gujarat follows with 22, Chhattisgarh and Rajasthan have 20 each, Uttar Pradesh has 14, Madhya Pradesh and Punjab have 12 each, and Jharkhand, Karnataka, Kerala, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, Uttarakhand and West Bengal have 10 each. The figures are provisional. NICL can raise or lower the count for any state and can cancel the process at any stage. Pick the state where you can actually report for a year of office training, because the posting goes where NICL sends you inside that state. NICL can move you to any district inside the state you pick, so choose the state, not a city in your head.</p>
<h2>No exam. Your graduation marks decide it</h2>
<p>There is no written test and no interview in this recruitment. The Board of Practical Training (Eastern Region) shortlists from the NATS portal data on the percentage of marks in the qualifying graduation examination, calculated to two decimal places. If your university gave you a CGPA or OGPA, convert it with the conversion formula your own university prescribes and enter the percentage in the NATS profile field. Applications without the percentage are not considered.</p>
<p>Shortlisted candidates hear through their registered email, then appear in person at the designated NICL office with original certificates and self-attested copies for document verification. Final engagement depends on eligibility, the seats left in the chosen state, and approval of the apprenticeship contract in NATS. If two candidates tie on the same percentage, the older candidate ranks higher. That makes the graduation percentage the whole contest. A candidate with 68 percent and a clean profile beats a candidate with 62 percent and a better resume, because the resume never enters the process. Check the percentage you entered in NATS before the window closes, since a wrong figure there is the one mistake no correction window can fix.</p>
<h2>How to apply on NATS without getting stuck</h2>
<p>The application lives entirely on the NATS portal, and it works in two stages. First, register as a student on nats.education.gov.in and complete the profile to 100 percent. Second, log in, go to "Apply against advertised vacancies", search for NATIONAL INSURANCE COMPANY LTD, and apply against the NICL vacancy. Creating a NATS account alone is not an application. You have to apply against the advertised vacancy itself.</p>
<p>NICL's establishment ID on NATS is EWBPNC096867, which helps if the name search throws up lookalikes. Apply for one state only. The portal takes no fee for this, because the notification names none, and you should pay nobody who claims to speed the process up. Keep the registered email active through the whole engagement, because document verification dates and every later instruction arrive there. The window is short: 14 days from 4 to 18 November. A half-finished NATS profile is the usual reason people miss it, since the profile takes a day of back and forth with documents and the vacancy does not wait.</p>
<p>Read the nationality and eligibility conditions in the official notification before applying, since this is an Indian public sector undertaking engagement. Apply only through the NATS portal, and never pay anyone who promises to get you a seat. Genuine recruitment never charges a fee to apply beyond what the notification itself names, and this notification names none.</p>
<h2>What the Rs 15,000 stipend does and does not mean</h2>
<p>The stipend is Rs 15,000 a month for the full 12 months. NICL pays Rs 8,850 of it and the government pays Rs 6,150 through direct benefit transfer to the apprentice's bank account. Apprentices get one day of leave per month of training, credited at the start of the following month, with a maximum of four days at a time. What the stipend does not carry matters as much as the figure. Apprentices are not NICL employees and get no employee allowances or benefits. NICL states plainly that it has no obligation to offer regular employment during or after the training, and the contract ends after 12 months. Completing the year does not create a claim to absorption.</p>
<p>So judge the year on its own terms. It is a year of paid office training inside a public-sector insurer, with a stipend that covers living costs in most of the 14 states, and a line on the resume that shows a year inside a government undertaking. It is not a back door into a permanent NICL job, and anyone selling it as one is selling something the notification does not promise. A similar opening runs alongside this one: <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/indian-bank-apprentice-1500-posts-20261106">Indian Bank has opened 1,500 apprentice seats</a> for the same financial year, also through NATS, with applications closing on 25 November. That one has an online exam, so it is worth comparing the two before you commit to one state in one of them. The notification gives no training start date. Shortlisted candidates are told the document verification arrangements by email, and the training start follows from that.</p>
<p><small>Sources: National Insurance Company Limited, Advertisement for Engagement of Apprentices for the year 2026-27, dated 4 November 2026, read 6 November 2026 from the official PDF on nationalinsurance.nic.co.in, as reported by JobCareerUpdates at https://jobcareerupdates.in/blog/nicl-apprentice-recruitment-2026/ and Testbook at https://testbook.com/news/nicl-apprentice-recruitment-2026/ on the seats, dates, stipend, eligibility conditions and marks-based selection. Apply only through nats.education.gov.in.</small></p>
